{
	title: 'ASON compatibility, performance and comment preservation',
	spec: "src/common/ason.ts starts from the original implementation in tasks/8/ason.ts, kept as an unchanged reference artifact. The live module may be edited and optimized; no format or API feature may be removed. Keep named exports and the default/named ason object: stringify, parse, parseAll, parseStream, COMMENTS, config and their types/options. stringify takes a mode string or { mode, indent }; indent is a string ('\\t', '  ') or a number of spaces, defaulting to the exported config.indent (a plain mutable object, '\\t' by default; per-call options win). Smart width counts characters: a tab is one column. -0 stringifies as -0 and round-trips, as in JS (JSON writes 0). Exact stringify output (quote choice, escapes, spacing, tabs, wrapping, comment modes) is a compatibility contract. Invalid input retains positional diagnostics and streaming retains its first-record behavior. Long strings parse by searching for delimiters/backslashes and slicing ordinary spans, not scanning each character; searches stay bounded to the current string and cached quote candidates avoid quadratic work on escape-heavy input. Short stringify does not scan its already newline-escaped inline output for newlines. Preserve both quote styles, backticks without interpolation, every escape and line continuation, and UTF-16 code units including surrogate pairs. The top comment is a self-contained guide, in this order: the selling point (a one-file, dependency-free drop-in for JSON and JSONL, readable and convenient out of the box, for config files, wire serialization and logs); a parse/stringify example with modes and indent/config; the philosophy (nearly every JS literal is valid ASON); not yet another JSON5/JSONC though a superset of JSON, JSONC and JSON5 syntax; a comment round-trip example and its limits; ASONL streaming, including parseStream dropping a malformed first line so it can start mid-stream; error format; the exact-output contract; and a verified TODO list of remaining differences from JSON/JS. Live ASON files edited by hand (config.ason, credentials and other liveFile data) parse with comments kept and write smart with COMMENTS intact. Leading comments on keys and array items survive value updates; comments in unsupported positions, replaced collections and short mode need not survive. Test config key/item comment preservation, exact-output compatibility and plausible slicing-boundary bugs.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"ason.ts started as a byte copy of tasks/8; task mw now allows compatible optimizations. ason.test.ts started as tasks/8/ason.test.ts and is pruned like any test: its tail -f e2e case (and the tail-file.ts helper only it used) is gone, so parseStream is tested with in-memory streams, and it is linted like other files. Hal's old extras live elsewhere: lines.encode (was ason.stringifyLine) and history.load's own line parsing with the torn-tail partial (was parseStream's midRecord/onPartial).",
		'live-file parses with { comments: true } and stringifies smart, so COMMENTS ride on the data objects: Object.assign and spread copy the symbol, a reload deletes it via Reflect.ownKeys. Replacing an array or object from code drops its comments; array comments are by index.',
		'Performance implementation: indexOf finds quote/backslash boundaries; searches for escapes are restricted to the remaining string, not the rest of the record (otherwise short strings repeatedly scan huge later fields). Cache closing quotes across escapes to avoid quadratic scans. Short stringify returns the inline collection without scanning for newlines: short string/key encoding already escapes them. Differential validation against the previous live implementation covered 20,000 seeded escape cases and every record of a generated 50.95 MB history, with identical parse results/errors and all three stringify modes. JSON5 strict superset is false: e.g. U+2000 separator whitespace is rejected by the original parser; do not promise it merely because the reference doc does.',
		"Invariants restored (2026-10-06): parse defines __proto__ as an own key, as JSON.parse does (no prototype pollution); an unclosed /* fails with 'Unterminated comment' at its start; stringify output holds no raw control characters, U+2028/U+2029 or lone surrogates (\\xHH/\\uHHHH escapes; \\r escaped in backticks too) and round-trips; templates read raw CR/CRLF as LF, as JS does; 0b/0o numbers, \\u{...} escapes and all JS whitespace parse. The header states exactly which comments survive: leading comments of keys and array items; all others are discarded."
